CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Act is to contribute to reducing greenhouse gas emissions and achieving carbon neutrality through the balanced development and utilization of marine space in a manner that reflects its public nature, strengthening the competitiveness of the offshore wind power industry, enhancing national and energy security, and developing the national economy, by prescribing matters regarding the systematic preparation of sites for offshore wind power generation to promote the deployment of offshore wind power and the support for the development of the offshore wind power industry and the creation of a sound ecosystem.

Article 2 (Definitions) The terms used in this Act are defined as follows: <Amended on Mar. 17, 2026>
  1. "Offshore wind power project" means a power generation project utilizing wind power under subparagraph 2b of Article 2 of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of New and Renewable Energy, in a sea area where water depth exists under subparagraph 3a of Article 2 of the Act on Marine Investigation and Use of Marine Information (referring to water depth measured based on the datum level under Article 8(1)2 of that Act) within the sea under subparagraph 1a of Article 2 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act or the beach under item b of that subparagraph;
  2. "Offshore wind power facilities" means offshore wind power equipment, facilities for an offshore wind power project for power transmission and transformation, and facilities appurtenant thereto;
  3. "Offshore wind power industry" means the industry related to the design, construction, operation, maintenance, decommissioning, and export of offshore wind power facilities, or the manufacturing and supply of components related thereto;
  4. "Preliminary offshore wind power generation zone" (hereinafter referred to as "preliminary zone") means an area designated and publicly notified under Article 14 as a candidate for designation as an offshore wind power generation zone, owing to its excellent conditions required for the installation of offshore wind power facilities, including wind conditions, environmental factors, and the minimization of negative impacts on local residents;
  5. "Basic design" means a fundamental design that determines the layout of offshore wind power facilities, the capacity of offshore wind power generators, and the interconnection with the electric power systems (referring to the electric power systems under subparagraph 14 of Article 2 of the Electric Utility Act; hereinafter the same shall apply), based on site information, wind conditions and other factors, in order to install offshore wind power facilities within a preliminary zone;
  6. "Offshore wind power generation zone" (hereinafter referred to as "power generation zone") means a zone designated and publicly notified under Article 19 for the purpose of collectively installing and operating offshore wind power facilities;
  7. "Offshore wind power project developer" means a person selected under Article 24 (1) to implement an offshore wind power project within a power generation zone;
  8. "Development implementation plan" (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plan") means an implementation plan formulated under Article 25 by an offshore wind power project developer to implement an offshore wind power project within a power generation zone;
  9. "Wind conditions" means all relevant information, including average wind speed, prevailing wind direction, wind power density, and turbulence intensity, required to calculate wind energy resources in a specific area.

Article 3 (Responsibilities of the State and local governments) (1) The State shall formulate various policy measures to promote the deployment and expansion of offshore wind power, such as actively securing sites in alignment with the direction of energy policies.
  (2) Where the State prepares sites for offshore wind power and promotes an offshore wind power project, it shall ensure that the project is harmonized with the conservation of the marine environment, the securing of marine safety, the fisheries industry, and other marine uses, thereby realizing the sustainable marine development and utilization.
  (3) The State shall provide various forms of support, such as establishing and providing offshore wind power information, to ensure that offshore wind power projects are effectively executed.
  (4) The State shall promote the offshore wind power industry, while exploring support measures for other industries that may be affected by offshore wind power.
  (5) The State shall endeavor to enhance public understanding of the installation and utilization of offshore wind power facilities through education, public relations, and other related activities.
  (6) Local governments shall formulate policy measures necessary to promote the offshore wind power industry and related regional industries and support local residents, taking into account national policies and regional characteristics.
  (7) The State and local governments shall cooperate with each other regarding the orderly deployment of offshore wind power, the securing of public acceptance, and the interconnection of offshore wind power with electric power systems, in order to ensure that the establishment and implementation of planned sites for offshore wind power are effectively executed.
  (8) The State and local governments shall formulate policy measures regarding power generation zones, etc., taking into account that offshore wind power is a public resource.
  (9) An offshore wind power project developer shall be aware of the public nature of public waters belonging to a power generation zone and conduct its business activities in a manner that ensures the conservation of the marine environment, the securing of marine safety, and the harmonious coexistence of the fisheries industry and other marine uses, and shall cooperate in the policy measures of the State and local governments.
  (10) The State and local governments shall endeavor to secure the financial resources necessary for promoting the policy measures under paragraphs (1) through (8).

Article 4 (Scope of application) This Act shall apply to offshore wind power projects and the installation of offshore wind power facilities; provided, wind power projects under Article 303 of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City shall be excluded herefrom.

Article 5 (Relationship to other statutes) (1) This Act shall prevail over other statutes regarding the designation of preliminary zones and power generation zones, the permission and authorization procedures for offshore wind power projects and the supervision thereof, and the installation of offshore wind power facilities.
  (2) Except as provided in this Act, the deployment of and support for offshore wind power shall be governed by the Act on Promotion of Development, Use and Diffusion of Renewable Energy. <Amended on Mar. 17, 2026>

CHAPTER II OFFSHORE WIND POWER COMMITTEE AND OFFSHORE WIND POWER TASK FORCE

Article 6 (Offshore Wind Power Committee) (1) The Offshore Wind Power Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established under the Prime Minister to deliberate on and resolve the following matters regarding offshore wind power:
  1. Matters regarding the designation, modified designation, and de-designation of preliminary zones under Articles 14 and 15;
  2. Matters regarding the finalization and modification of the basic design under Article 16;
  3. Matters regarding the designation, modified designation, and de-designation of power generation zones under Articles 19 and 21;
  4. Matters regarding the selection, and revocation of selection, of offshore wind power project developers under Article 24;
  5. Matters regarding the approval, modified approval, and revocation of approval, of implementation plans under Articles 25 and 28;
  6. Matters regarding the provision of data necessary for the preparation of implementation plans under Article 25(2);
  7. Matters regarding decisions, permission, authorization, designation, approval, reporting, consultation, and de-designation under this Act;
  8. Matters regarding the coordination of differing opinions among competent administrative agencies related to the installation of offshore wind power facilities and the creation of power generation zones;
  9. Matters regarding dispute mediation with respect to the designation of preliminary zones and power generation zones, and to offshore wind power projects;
  10. Matters regarding the improvement of statutes, systems, and policies relating to offshore wind power projects and the installation of offshore wind power facilities;
  11. Other matters deemed necessary by the chairperson and referred to the Committee.
  (2) Where the Committee has deliberated on and resolved the matters under paragraph (1)10, it may recommend that the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and local governments make improvements thereto. In such cases, the heads of relevant central administrative agencies and local governments shall comply therewith, unless there are justifiable grounds.

Article 7 (Composition and operation of Committee) (1) The Committee shall be composed of not more than 25 members, including two chairpersons, and the office of chairperson shall be jointly assumed by the following persons:
  1. The Prime Minister;
  2. One person designated by the President, from among the members commissioned under paragraph (2)2.
  (2) The Committee shall consist of the following ex officio members and commissioned members: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Ex officio members: The Minister of Finance and Economy, the Minister of National Defense, the Minister of the Interior and Safety, the Minister of Agriculture, Food and Rural Affairs, the Minister of Trade, Industry and Resources, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, the Minister of Land, Infrastructure and Transport, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, the Minister of Planning and Budget, the Administrator of the National Heritage Administration, and the heads of central administrative agencies prescribed by Presidential Decree;
  2. Commissioned members: Persons commissioned by the Prime Minister from among persons with extensive knowledge of and experience in wind power, including such fields as energy and resources, environment and marine environment, fisheries, maritime traffic, land use, science and technology, electric power systems, national defense, accounting and finance, and conflict management.
  (3) In commissioning members under paragraph (2)2, efforts shall be made to reflect the representativeness of each field, after receiving recommendations of candidates or hearing opinions from academia, business, civil society organizations, or local governments.
  (4) To ensure the efficient operation of the Committee, one executive secretary shall be appointed, the position of which shall be filled by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Each chairperson shall independently represent the Committee and exercise overall control over its business affairs.
  (6) If both chairpersons are unable to perform their duties due to unavoidable reasons, a member pre-designated by the chairperson who is the Prime Minister shall act as the chairperson.
  (7) The term of office for commissioned members shall be 2 years, and they may be reappointed; provided, the term of office for a member newly commissioned due to resignation of a member, etc. shall be the remaining term of his or her predecessor.
  (8) A majority of the members of the Committee shall constitute a quorum to convene a meeting (including a video conference), and any resolution thereof shall require the concurring vote of a majority of those present; provided, in cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where the subject matter of the agenda item is minor, the Committee may deliberate on and resolve the agenda item in writing.
  (9) A working committee shall be established under the Committee to efficiently perform the following matters; and in such cases, the deliberation and resolution by the working committee under subparagraph 2 shall be deemed the deliberation and resolution by the Committee:
  1. Review and coordination of agenda items to be deliberated on by the Committee;
  2. Deliberation on and resolution of minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree, among the matters referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 6(1).
  (10) Other matters necessary for the composition and operation of the Committee and the working committee shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 8 (Grounds for disqualification of members) (1) Any of the following persons shall be disqualified to be a member of the Committee:
  1. A person under adult guardianship;
  2. A person whose qualification has been suspended by statutes or a court ruling;
  3. A person for whom 3 years have not passed since the execution of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment was completed (including cases where the execution is deemed completed) or exempted;
  4. A person under suspension of the execution of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment declared by a court.
  (2) No member of the Committee shall be dismissed or removed from office against his or her will, except in any of the following cases:
  1. Where the member is sentenced to imprisonment without labor or a heavier punishment;
  2. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties for a long period due to a mental or physical weakness.

Article 9 (Exclusion of, challenge to, or recusal by members) (1) A member shall be excluded from the deliberation on and resolution of the relevant agenda item if he or she falls under any of the following cases:
  1. Where the member, or a person who is or was his or her spouse, is a party to the relevant matter, or is a joint holder of rights or an obligor with respect to such case;
  2. Where the member is or was a relative of a party to the relevant matter;
  3. Where the member has provided testimony, appraisal, or legal advice with respect to the relevant matter;
  4. Where the member is or has been involved in the relevant matter as an agent for a party;
  5. Where the member, or a public institution, corporation, or organization to which the member belongs, has a conflict of interest with a person to whom the member or the entity provides advice or other support.
  (2) If circumstances indicate that it is difficult to expect fair deliberation and resolution from a member, a party may file an application for challenge, which shall be decided on by a resolution of the Committee. In such cases, the member of the Committee subject to the application for challenge shall not participate in the resolution.
  (3) If a member of the Committee falls under any subparagraph of paragraph (1), he or she shall notify the Committee thereof and voluntarily recuse himself or herself from the deliberation on and resolution of the relevant agenda item.
  (4) Where an ex officio member falls under any of the grounds for exclusion, challenge, or recusal under paragraphs (1) through (3), a person separately designated by such member may act on his or her behalf.

Article 10 (Establishment of task force) (1) To support the smooth performance of business affairs of the Committee, the offshore wind power task force (hereinafter referred to as the "task force") shall be established under the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where necessary for the operation of the task force, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the heads of relevant central administrative agencies, local governments, and related institutions, corporations, or organizations (hereinafter referred to as "relevant institutions") to second public officials, officers, or employees under their jurisdiction. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Other matters necessary for the composition and operation of the task force shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 11 (Designation of dedicated offshore wind power institution) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate a dedicated institution for offshore wind power (hereinafter referred to as "dedicated institution") to efficiently execute policies regarding the development of the offshore wind power industry. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The dedicated institution shall perform the following: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Projects necessary for the establishment and operation of the offshore wind power site information network;
  2. Projects necessary for the creation and management of preliminary zones and power generation zones;
  3. Projects necessary for the selection and management of offshore wind power project developers;
  4. Projects for providing support to offshore wind power project developers;
  5. Projects for the education and training of human resources for the offshore wind power industry;
  6. Projects for the analysis of trends and the compilation of statistics regarding offshore wind power;
  7. Other projects entrusted by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment.
  (3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may contribute or subsidize expenses necessary for operating the dedicated institution or performing the projects listed in the subparagraphs of paragraph (2). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where the dedicated institution falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the designation thereof; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, such designation shall be revoked: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it fails to perform its duties as a dedicated institution without justifiable grounds;
  3. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation as a dedicated institution under paragraph (5);
  4. Where it becomes significantly inappropriate or unfair for the dedicated institution to perform its duties as such.
  (5) Other matters necessary for the criteria and procedures for the designation, or revocation of designation, of the dedicated institution, as well as the management and supervision thereof, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER III DESIGNATION OF PRELIMINARY ZONES AND POWER GENERATION ZONES

Article 12 (Establishment and operation of offshore wind power site information network) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall jointly establish and operate an offshore wind power site information network to promptly collect and analyze information prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as wind conditions, fishing activities, environment and marine environment, maritime traffic, military operations impact, national heritage impact, and information on electric power systems, which are necessary for the designation of preliminary zones and power generation zones. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the heads of relevant agencies to provide data or information necessary for the establishment and operation of the offshore wind power site information network. In such cases, the heads of relevant agencies so requested shall comply unless there are special circumstances. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The heads of relevant central administrative agencies may conduct relevant surveys to provide data and information necessary for the establishment and operation of the offshore wind power site information network.
  (4) Other matters necessary for the establishment and operation of the offshore wind power site information network shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 13 (Vesting of ownership of data on offshore wind power site surveys) (1) Data related to marine exploration and survey findings and marine site information for offshore wind power projects shall vest in the State.
  (2) Even where a person other than the State acquires the data referred to in paragraph (1) in the course of executing an offshore wind power project, the ownership of such data shall vest in the State.

Article 14 (Designation of preliminary zones) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may designate an area that meets all of the following requirements as a preliminary zone by utilizing the offshore wind power site information network under Article 12, subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee; and in such cases, a wind power generation installations plan contained in the basic plan for electricity supply and demand under Article 25 of the Electricity Utility Act shall be taken into consideration: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Having wind conditions suitable for offshore wind power generation;
  2. Having a minimal impact on fishing activities;
  3. Not posing an impediment to maritime traffic safety;
  4. Not interfering with the use and operation of harbors and fishing ports;
  5. Having a minimal impact on the marine environment and ecosystem;
  6. Having a minimal impact on the conduct of military operations;
  7. Meeting such other requirements as prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may designate an area that meets all of the requirements specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) as a preliminary zone, upon application by the competent Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor") and subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intend to modify any significant matter prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the location or area of a preliminary zone, they shall modify the designation of the preliminary zone subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designates a preliminary zone under paragraph (1) or modifies the designation thereof under paragraph (3), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Other matters necessary for the designation and modified designation of a preliminary zone shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 15 (De-designation of preliminary zones) (1) Where the purpose of the designation of a preliminary zone has been achieved or is deemed unachievable, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may de-designate it, subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment de-designates a preliminary zone under paragraph (1), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where all or part of a preliminary zone is designated as a power generation zone under Article 19, the preliminary zone shall be deemed to have been de-designated on the date of such designation as a power generation zone.
  (4) Other matters necessary for the de-designation of a preliminary zone shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 16 (Formulation and finalization of basic design) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall formulate a draft basic design including the following matters for a preliminary zone, and finalize the basic design subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee; and the same shall apply to any modification of significant matters prescribed by Presidential Decree among the matters in the finalized basic design: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Name, location, and area of the preliminary zone;
  2. Layout and capacity of offshore wind power facilities;
  3. Methods of implementing offshore wind power projects;
  4. Plans for electric power system interconnection and major infrastructure;
  5. Plans for preliminary surveys regarding impacts on the marine environment, maritime traffic safety, military operations, and national heritage;
  6. Plans for stakeholder acceptance within planned-site areas;
  7. Other matters regarding preliminary zones prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the heads of relevant agencies to provide data or information necessary for formulating a draft basic design or modifying the finalized basic design under paragraph (1). In such cases, the heads of relevant agencies so requested shall comply unless there are special circumstances. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to formulate a draft basic design under paragraph (1) or modify any significant matter in the finalized basic design, he or she shall conduct a marine environmental impact survey as prescribed by Presidential Decree and request consultations with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries; provided, where the draft basic design to be formulated or any significant matter in the finalized basic design to be modified includes an area other than public waters under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall conduct an environmental impact survey regarding such area other than public waters, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Upon receipt of a request for consultations under paragraph (3), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall provide his or her opinion within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree; and in such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall reflect such opinion in the draft basic design or the basic design to be modified, unless there are special circumstances. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to formulate a draft basic design under paragraph (1) or modify any significant matter in the finalized basic design, he or she shall notify a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (referring to the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) who establishes and operates a public-private council under Article 17(1). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment finalizes or modifies the basic design under paragraph (1), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 17 (Establishment and operation of public-private council) (1) A Mayor/Do Governor (where a preliminary zone extends over the maritime areas under the jurisdiction of two or more local governments under subparagraph 2 of Article 2(1) of the Local Autonomy Act within the jurisdiction of the same local government under subparagraph 1 of that paragraph) or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu (where a preliminary zone is located within the maritime area under the jurisdiction of a single local government under Article 2(1)2 of the Local Autonomy Act) shall establish and operate a public-private council in which stakeholders, including fishers' organizations, resident representatives, relevant experts, and representatives of residents in areas along transmission lines, participate to consult on the following matters; provided, where a preliminary zone is located within the exclusive economic zone under the Act on the Exclusive Economic Zone and Continental Shelf or extends over the maritime areas under the jurisdiction of two or more local governments under Article 2(1)1 of the Local Autonomy Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall, after consultation with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries and subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee, determine the Mayor/Do Governor who shall organize and operate the public-private council: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Matters regarding the draft basic design;
  2. Matters regarding the designation, modified designation, and de-designation of power generation zones;
  3. Matters regarding the organization and operation of profit-sharing projects with resident participation, including nearby residents or fishers of preliminary zones and power generation zones;
  4. Matters regarding the revitalization of the fisheries industry and local economy;
  5. Other matters necessary for securing stakeholder acceptance.
  (2) The Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall organize and operate a public-private council as prescribed by Presidential Decree within 3 months from the date of receiving the notification under Article 16(5). In such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may provide necessary support as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where a public-private council is not organized within the period under paragraph (2), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu to organize and operate the public-private council. In such cases, the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu so requested shall comply unless there are special circumstances. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Prior to consultations by the public-private council, a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, make the draft basic design available for review by stakeholders, including fishers and residents, and relevant experts, and shall hold a public hearing or a briefing session to hear their opinions.
  (5) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall notify the Committee of the results of consultations by the public-private council within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree; provided, where it is unfeasible to notify such consultation results, the notification deadline may be extended once in consultation with the Committee.
  (6) A Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may request the Committee to mediate a dispute if dispute mediation, etc. is required in the course of consultation on the matters under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1). In such cases, the Committee shall mediate the dispute as prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 18 (Resident participation and profit-sharing in offshore wind power projects) (1) Residents of an area where offshore wind power facilities are installed within a power generation zone, and fishers whose fishing activities are affected by such installation, may participate in an offshore wind power project of the relevant area pursuant to Article 27-2 of the Act on the Promotion of the Development, Use and Diffusion of Renewable Energy. In such cases, financial support may be provided to the participating residents. <Amended on Mar. 17, 2026>
  (2) Preferential treatment may be given to fishers under paragraph (1) in terms of matters such as the scale of their investment in the offshore wind power project.
  (3) The scope of residents and fishers subject to participation under paragraph (1), the scale of preferential treatment under paragraph (2), and other necessary matters shall be determined by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 19 (Designation of power generation zones) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate all or part of a preliminary zone that meets all of the following requirements and has undergone consultation with the public-private council under Article 17 as a power generation zone, subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Having wind conditions that ensure economic viability;
  2. Being feasible to develop the site and infrastructure for the power generation zone;
  3. Being feasible to secure stakeholder acceptance and develop an environmentally friendly power generation zone;
  4. Being feasible to contribute to strengthening the offshore wind power industry ecosystem;
  5. Considering electric power system interconnection;
  6. Meeting other requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to modify any significant matter prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the location or area of a power generation zone designated under paragraph (1), he or she shall modify the designation of the power generation zone after consultation with the public-private council under Article 17 and deliberation and resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Prior to designating a power generation zone, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall request a transmission business entity under subparagraph 6 of Article 2 of the Electric Utility Act (hereinafter referred to as "transmission business entity") to confirm the electric power system interconnection for the power generation zone. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment designates a power generation zone under paragraph (1) or modifies the designation thereof under paragraph (2), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) Where the head of a relevant administrative agency intends to grant permission or make any other disposition regarding any of the following matters, or directly conduct a project within a power generation zone after the designation and public notice of the power generation zone, he or she shall consult with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Installation of roads, railroads, bridges, canals, waterworks, waterways, etc. and their appurtenances;
  2. Diversion of river streams, reclamation and dredging of rivers or sea surfaces, construction or alteration of ports, and the establishment of fishing rights or aquaculture rights;
  3. Erection, enlargement, or alteration of buildings;
  4. Clearing of land, excavation or reclamation of ground, and other alterations to the form and quality of land.
  (6) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may provide necessary administrative and financial support to ensure the smooth implementation of offshore wind power projects within power generation zones. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (7) Other matters necessary for the designation of power generation zones, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 20 (Interconnection of electric power systems for power generation zones) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request a transmission business entity to install connection equipment jointly used by multiple offshore wind power project developers (referring to equipment prescribed by Presidential Decree among electric installations defined in subparagraph 16 of Article 2 of the Electric Utility Act; hereinafter referred to as "joint connection equipment") in a power generation zone of at least a certain scale prescribed by Presidential Decree. In such cases, the transmission business entity shall comply unless there are special circumstances prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Pursuant to Article 15(1) of the Electricity Utility Act, a transmission business entity may charge an offshore wind power project developer who accesses joint connection equipment to help cover the costs of installation, operation, and maintenance of such joint connection equipment.

Article 21 (De-designation of power generation zones) (1) Where the purpose of the designation of a power generation zone has been achieved or is deemed unachievable, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may de-designate it, subject to deliberation and resolution by the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment de-designates a power generation zone under paragraph (1), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 22 (Special cases concerning preliminary feasibility study) Where deemed necessary for the prompt and smooth implementation of offshore wind power projects within a power generation zone, the Minister of Planning and Budget may exempt the projects from a preliminary feasibility study, notwithstanding Article 38 (1) of the National Finance Act. In such cases, he or she shall report the details of and grounds for such exemption to the competent standing committee of the National Assembly without delay. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 23 (Special cases concerning preliminary feasibility study for public enterprises and quasi-governmental institutions) (1) The head of a public enterprise or quasi-governmental institution (hereafter referred to as "head of an institution" in this Article) may apply to the Minister of Finance and Economy for an exemption from a preliminary feasibility study, notwithstanding the main clause of Article 40(3) of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, with respect to an offshore wind power project deemed to require a particularly prompt and smooth implementation within a power generation zone. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Upon receiving an application for an exemption from a preliminary feasibility study under paragraph (1), the Minister of Finance and Economy shall determine whether to grant the exemption after consultation with relevant experts, and notify the head of an institution of the results thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) If necessary for securing the financial soundness of a public institution and efficiently implementing a project, the Minister of Finance and Economy may have the head of an institution review the adequacy of the project plan, including an analysis of financing plans, total project costs, and efficient alternatives, with respect to a project exempt from a preliminary feasibility study under paragraph (2). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Other matters necessary for the procedures, etc. for a preliminary feasibility study for projects of public enterprises and quasi-governmental institutions shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

CHAPTER IV SELECTION OF OFFSHORE WIND POWER PROJECT DEVELOPERS, AND AUTHORIZATION OR PERMISSION

Article 24 (Selection of offshore wind power project developers) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may select an offshore wind power project developer after deliberation and resolution by the Committee, taking into account the following matters; and in such cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may give preferential treatment to a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions that owns a coal-fired power plant with a capacity of at least 200 megawatts: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Capability to efficiently conduct an offshore wind power generation project, including the cost of power generation;
  2. Financial soundness and financing capability;
  3. Efforts for mutually beneficial cooperation with stakeholders and to secure their acceptance, including profit-sharing;
  4. Contribution to securing industrial competitiveness and developing the offshore wind power industry;
  5. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree for the smooth implementation of an offshore wind power generation project.
  (2) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to select an offshore wind power project developer pursuant to paragraph (1), he or she shall publish an invitation for bids, including the following matters: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Location and area of the power generation zone subject to bidding;
  2. Validity period of the power generation zone subject to bidding;
  3. Qualification criteria for bidders;
  4. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (3) In any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the selection of an offshore wind power project developer or take any other necessary measures after deliberation and resolution by the Committee; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke the selection: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where the developer has been selected by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the developer no longer meets the requirements specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1);
  3. Where the developer violates this Act, and it is deemed necessary to revoke the selection thereof for the public interest;
  4. Where the developer fails to comply with the implementation plan approved pursuant to Article 25 without justifiable grounds;
  5. Where the developer fails to apply for approval of the implementation plan under Article 25(1) within 2 years from the date of selection as an offshore wind power project developer without justifiable grounds; provided, where the developer is a public institution under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions, the period required for the preparation and conduct of a preliminary feasibility study under Article 40(3) of that Act shall not be included in the period;
  6. Where the developer fails to commence construction within 3 years from the date of obtaining approval of the implementation plan under Article 25(1) without justifiable grounds.
  (4) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment revokes the selection of an offshore wind power project developer pursuant to paragraph (3), he or she may select a new offshore wind power project developer without delay. In such cases, paragraphs (1) through (3) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the selection of a new offshore wind power project developer and the revocation of such selection. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) An offshore wind power project developer selected under paragraph (4) shall succeed to the status of the former offshore wind power project developer regarding the approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1).
  (6) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment selects an offshore wind power project developer or revokes the selection under paragraphs (1), (3) and (4), he or she shall give public notice thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (7) Other matters necessary for the selection of an offshore wind power project developer and revocation of such selection shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 25 (Approval of implementation plan) (1) An offshore wind power project developer shall formulate an implementation plan including the following matters and obtain approval from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment after deliberation and resolution by the Committee; and in such cases, the offshore wind power project developer may divide a power generation zone into two or more sections to formulate a phased implementation plan and obtain approval thereof: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Overview of offshore wind power facilities;
  2. Location and area of the offshore wind power project site;
  3. Implementation period of the offshore wind power project;
  4. Matters regarding funds required for the implementation of the offshore wind power project and financing for the project;
  5. Matters regarding the installation of public facilities, such as roads, bridges, and ports, related to the offshore wind power project and the bearing of associated costs;
  6. Matters regarding the conservation of the marine environment and ecosystems;
  7. Matters regarding the implementation of the results of consultation with the public-private council;
  8. Matters regarding compensation for losses on fishing rights or aquaculture rights;
  9. Matters regarding security assurance and disaster preparedness for offshore wind power equipment;
  10. Matters regarding the implementation of project certification related to offshore wind power equipment;
  11. Matters regarding contributions to securing industrial competitiveness and developing the offshore wind power industry;
  12. Other matters regarding the execution of an implementation plan prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where necessary for an offshore wind power project developer to formulate an implementation plan under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may request the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries to provide site information data, such as geological survey data on the power generation zone. In such cases, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries so requested may provide the data within the scope necessary for the offshore wind power project developer to formulate the implementation plan, and the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall provide such data to the relevant offshore wind power project developer. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) When filing an application for approval of an implementation plan under paragraph (1), an offshore wind power project developer shall have the following documents attached:
  1. An environmental assessment report under Article 26(1);
  2. Documents submitted for authorization, permission, etc. referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 27(1).
  (4) Where an offshore wind power project developer intends to modify any matter approved under paragraph (1), the developer shall obtain approval for modification from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment after deliberation and resolution by the Committee; provided, in the case of modification of any minor matter prescribed by Presidential Decree, the developer shall file a report thereon with the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall have a prior consultation with a Mayor/Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the relevant power generation zone (referring to the Mayor/Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu who organizes and operates a public-private council under Article 17(1); hereafter the same shall apply in this paragraph) and with the heads of relevant administrative agencies prior to deliberation and resolution by the Committee under paragraph (1) or (4). In such cases, the Mayor/Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu, and the heads of relevant administrative agencies, in receipt of a request for consultation, shall submit their opinions within the period prescribed by Presidential Decree from the date of receipt of such request, and if they fail to do so within such period, consultation shall be deemed to have been conducted. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Where deliberation and resolution by the Committee under paragraph (1) or (4) have been conducted, deliberation by any of the following respective committees shall be deemed to have been undergone or conducted: <Amended on Apr. 28, 2026>
  1. Landscape Committee under the Landscape Act;
  2. Urban Planning Committee under the National Land Planning and Utilization Act;
  3. National Heritage Committee under the Framework Act on National Heritage;
  4. Mountainous District Management Committee under the Mountainous Districts Management Act;
  5. Disaster Impact Assessment Deliberation Committee under the Countermeasures Against Natural Disasters Act;
  6. Natural Scenery Deliberation Committee under the Natural Environment Conservation Act;
  7. Electricity Regulatory Commission under the Electric Utility Act.
  (7) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment approves an implementation plan pursuant to paragraph (1) or approves a modification thereof pursuant to paragraph (4), he or she shall give public notice thereof in the Official Gazette as prescribed by Presidential Decree and send copies of the relevant documents to the Mayor/Do Governor and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the relevant power generation zone. In such cases, the Mayor/Do Governor and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall make the copies of the relevant documents received available for public review for at least 14 days. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (8) Where the relevant documents, copies of which have been received, include matters regarding a decision on an urban or Gun management plan under Article 2(4) of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, the competent Mayor/Do Governor and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall take necessary measures, such as applying for approval of topographic maps pursuant to Article 32 (2) of that Act. In such cases, an offshore wind power project developer shall submit documents necessary for public notice of topographic maps, etc. to the competent Mayor/Do Governor and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
  (9) Other matters necessary for approval of an implementation plan, etc. shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 26 (Special cases concerning environmental impact assessment and marine use impact assessment) (1) Where an offshore wind power project developer applies for approval of an implementation plan, the developer shall conduct an environmental assessment and prepare an environmental assessment report as prescribed by Presidential Decree and submit the same to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, notwithstanding Article 22 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act and Article 13 of the Marine Use Impact Assessment Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall request consultation with the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries regarding the environmental assessment report submitted pursuant to paragraph (1). In such cases, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries so requested shall submit his or her opinions as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where opinions are submitted pursuant to paragraph (2), consultations on an environmental impact assessment under Article 22 of the Environmental Impact Assessment Act and consultations on a marine use impact assessment under Article 13 of the Marine Use Impact Assessment Act shall be deemed to have been completed.

Article 27 (Authorization and permission deemed granted under other statutes) (1) Where an offshore wind power project developer obtains approval or modified approval of an implementation plan in a power generation zone under Article 25, the following approval, permission, authorization, reporting, designation, decision, license, consultation, cancellation, deliberation, disposition, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "authorization, permission, etc.") shall be deemed to have been obtained; and when the implementation plan is publicly notified pursuant to Article 25(7), authorization, permission, etc. under the following statutes shall be deemed to have been publicly notified or announced:
  1. Deliberation on landscapes of development projects under Article 27 of the Landscape Act and landscapes of buildings under Article 28 of that Act;
  2. Permission for occupancy or use of public waters under Article 8 of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act, approval of or reporting on an implementation plan for occupancy or use under Article 17 of that Act, a license for reclamation of public waters under Article 28 of that Act, consultation on or approval of reclamation implemented by the State, etc. under Article 35 of that Act, and approval of an implementation plan for reclamation of public waters under Article 38 of that Act;
  3. A disposition of non-permission under Article 24 of the Mining Act, and a disposition of revocation of a mining right or a disposition of reduction of a mining area under Article 34 of that Act;
  4. Consultation based on the impact assessment results under Article 9 of the Act on the Assessment of Impacts on National Heritage;
  5. Permission for the lease or use of State forests under Article 21 of the State Forest Administration and Management Act;
  6. Determination of an urban or Gun management plan under Article 30 of the National Land Planning and Utilization Act, permission for development activities under Article 56 of that Act, designation of an urban or Gun planning facility project operator under Article 86 of that Act, and authorization for an implementation plan under Article 88 of that Act;
  7. Consultation on dispositions by administrative agencies under Article 13 of the Protection of Military Bases and Installations Act;
  8. Permission for road occupancy and use under Article 18 of the Act on the Maintenance and Improvement of Road Networks in Agricultural and Fishing Villages;
  9. Permission for use of agricultural infrastructure under Article 23 of the Agricultural and Fishing Villages Improvement Act;
  10. Permission for, or consultation on, the diversion of farmland under Article 34 of the Farmland Act, reporting on the diversion of farmland under Article 35 of that Act, and permission for, or consultation on, the temporary use of farmland for other purposes under Article 36 of that Act;
  11. Permission for the execution of road works by a person other than road management authorities under Article 36 of the Road Act, permission for the connection of a road with other facilities under Article 52 of that Act, and permission for the occupancy of a road under Article 61 of that Act;
  12. Permission for an act in an urban development area under Article 9(5) of the Urban Development Act;
  13. Permission for building private roads under Article 4 of the Private Road Act;
  14. Permission for the felling of trees, etc. under Article 14 of the Erosion Control Work Act and the cancellation of designation of erosion control land under Article 20 of that Act;
  15. Permission for, or reporting on, the felling of standing trees, etc. under Article 36(1) and (5) of the Creation and Management of Forest Resources Act;
  16. Permission for conversion of mountainous districts under Article 14 of the Mountainous Districts Management Act, permission for, or reporting on, temporary use of mountainous districts under Article 15-2 of that Act, and permission for extracting soil and stone under Article 25 of that Act;
  17. Authorization for the installation of private-use waterworks and private-use industrial waterworks under Articles 52 and 54 of the Water Supply and Waterworks Installation Act;
  18. Approval of an act within a wetland protected area under Article 13(5)3 of the Wetlands Conservation Act;
  19. Permission for an act within a park area under Article 23 of the Natural Parks Act;
  20. Consultation on disaster impact assessments, etc. under Article 4 of the Countermeasures Against Natural Disasters Act;
  21. Permission for reburial under Article 27(1) of the Act on Funeral Services;
  22. License for an electric utility business under Article 7 of the Electric Utility Act and authorization for the acquisition, etc. of an electric utility business under Article 10 of that Act;
  23. Permission for alterations to the land form and quality, etc. on grassland under Article 21-2 of the Grassland Act and permission for conversion of grassland under Article 23 of that Act;
  24. Permission for the execution of river works under Article 30 of the River Act, permission for the occupancy of a river under Article 33 of that Act, and permission for the use of river water under Article 50 of that Act;
  25. Permission for the installation and management of aids to navigation under Article 9(6) or Article 13(1) of the Aids to Navigation Act;
  26. Permission for the implementation of a harbor development project under Article 9(2) of the Harbor Act and approval of an implementation plan for a harbor development project under Article 10(2) of that Act;
  27. Sea traffic safety assessments under Article 13 of the Sea Traffic Safety Act;
  28. Designation of an energy development zone among marine use zones under Article 12 of the Act on Marine Spatial Planning and Management.
  (2) An offshore wind power project developer applying for approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1) or a modified approval under paragraph (4) of that Article shall also submit the relevant documents prescribed by the applicable statutes under which authorization, permission, etc. are deemed to have been granted under paragraph (1).
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment intends to grant approval for an implementation plan including the matters specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) under Article 25(1) or grant a modified approval under paragraph (4) of that Article, he or she shall have a prior consultation with the heads of relevant administrative agencies. In such cases, the head of a relevant administrative agency so requested shall submit his or her opinions within 30 days from the date of receiving such request, and if he or she fails to do so within that period, consultation shall be deemed to have been held. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where an offshore wind power project developer intends to construct a building under Article 2(1)2 of the Building Act in order to execute an offshore wind power project for which approval or modified approval of an implementation plan has been obtained pursuant to Article 25(1) or (4), the developer shall be deemed to have obtained a building permit or made a building report under Article 11 or 14 of that Act by submitting basic design documents under Article 11(2) of that Act to the head of the relevant administrative agency.
  (5) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment grants approval or modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(1) or (4), he or she shall notify the head of the relevant administrative agency of the details thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 28 (Revocation of approval of implementation plan) (1) Where an offshore wind power project developer who has obtained approval of an implementation plan falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may, after deliberation and resolution by the Committee, order the revocation or modification of an order or disposition regarding the power generation zone development project under this Act, order the suspension of construction, or order other necessary measures; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, the Minister shall revoke the approval of the implementation plan: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where the developer has obtained approval of an implementation plan by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where the developer fails to commence the power generation zone development project within 3 years from the date of approval of the implementation plan without justifiable grounds;
  3. Where it is deemed difficult to achieve the purpose of the power generation zone development project due to natural disasters, the bankruptcy of the developer, or other reasons prescribed by Presidential Decree.
  (2) Where the approval of an implementation plan is revoked under paragraph (1), any authorization, permission, etc. deemed granted or obtained under the subparagraphs of Article 27(1) shall become invalid, and the implementation plan, etc. formulated or modified shall be deemed reverted to its state prior to the approval of the relevant implementation plan or shall be deemed abolished.
  (3) Where the approval of an implementation plan is revoked under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall give public notice thereof in the Official Gazette as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and notify the competent Mayor/Do Governor and the head of the Si/Gun/Gu thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 29 (Expropriation and use of land) (1) An offshore wind power project developer may expropriate or use land, buildings, other objects fixed to land, rights other than ownership thereto, mining rights, fishing rights, aquaculture rights, and rights to use water (hereinafter referred to as "land, etc.") necessary for an offshore wind power project.
  (2) Where a public notice of the approval or modified approval of an implementation plan under Article 25(7) is given, the project approval and the public notice of project approval under Articles 20 and 22 of the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects shall be deemed to have been granted and given.
  (3) Except as provided in this Act, the Act on Acquisition of and Compensation for Land for Public Works Projects shall apply mutatis mutandis to the expropriation or use of land, etc. under paragraph (1).

Article 30 (Report on commencement of construction) (1) Where an offshore wind power project developer intends to commence construction for a development project in accordance with an implementation plan or suspend construction for at least 3 months, the developer shall report the details thereof to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where an offshore wind power project developer reports the commencement of construction under paragraph (1), the developer shall be deemed to have obtained authorization for, or filed a report on, a construction plan for electric installations for electric utility under Article 61 of the Electric Utility Act.
  (3) Upon receiving a report pursuant to paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall report the details thereof to the Committee. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 31 (Authorization for completion) (1) When an offshore wind power project developer has completed a development project for a power generation zone in accordance with an implementation plan, the developer shall apply to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment for authorization for completion without delay. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Upon receiving an application for authorization for completion under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall conduct a completion inspection without delay and grant authorization for completion immediately if the construction work has been completed in compliance with the implementation plan; provided, where the construction work has not been completed in compliance with such plan, the Minister shall order that necessary measures be taken, such as supplementary construction, without delay. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment grants authorization for completion under the main clause of paragraph (2), he or she shall report such fact to the Committee, give public notice thereof as prescribed by Presidential Decree, and notify the offshore wind power project developer thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) An offshore wind power project developer who has obtained authorization for completion under paragraph (2) shall be deemed to have undergone a completion inspection or have obtained authorization for completion, etc. for the relevant project under the authorization, permission, etc. deemed granted by approval of an implementation plan pursuant to Article 27(1).
  (5) An offshore wind power project developer shall not use power generation facilities and equipment installed through a power generation zone development project before obtaining authorization for completion under paragraph (2); provided, this shall not apply where permission for use has been obtained from the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Where an offshore wind power project developer has obtained approval of an implementation plan for individual sections under the latter part of Article 25(1), the developer may apply for authorization for completion for each section.

Article 32 (Use of wind measuring devices) (1) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment has conducted in advance development activities required to be performed by an offshore wind power project developer, such as installing wind measuring devices, in the process of creating a power generation zone, the developer shall pay the costs thereof. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Matters necessary for those subject to the imposition of costs under paragraph (1), and the procedures and payment methods thereof, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 33 (Prohibition on electric utility licenses) (1) Where a person who intends to newly conduct an offshore wind power project in an area other than a preliminary zone or a power generation zone applies for an electric utility license under Article 7(1) of the Electric Utility Act, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment or a Mayor/Do Governor shall not grant such license. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where a person who intends to newly install wind measuring devices for the purpose of an offshore wind power project applies for permission for the occupancy or use of public waters under Article 8(1) of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of a local government managing the relevant public waters under Article 4(2) of that Act shall neither grant such permission nor accept such application; provided, this shall not apply where an offshore wind power project developer installs them in a power generation zone.
  [Effective Date: Mar. 26, 2028]

CHAPTER V PROMOTION OF OFFSHORE WIND POWER INDUSTRY AND SUPPORT FOR AFFECTED INDUSTRIES

Article 34 (Facilitation of development of offshore wind power technology) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall promote the following matters to facilitate the development of offshore wind power technology: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Technology trends and demand surveys for offshore wind power;
  2. Research, development, and evaluation of offshore wind power technologies;
  3. Securing of rights to and commercialization of developed technologies;
  4. Standardization of offshore wind power equipment, components, etc.;
  5. Exchange of information on offshore wind technologies;
  6. Other matters necessary for the research and development of offshore wind power technologies.
  (2) In order to facilitate the development of offshore wind power technologies under paragraph (1), the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may provide financial support to a person who conducts research and development on relevant technologies or commercializes them to cover the associated costs. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall formulate measures to systematically and comprehensively manage and disseminate information on offshore wind power technologies. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 35 (Fostering of specialized human resources) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish and implement policies necessary for fostering specialized human resources and improving technologies related to the offshore wind power industry. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may conduct education and training for human resources specialized in the offshore wind power industry. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Matters necessary for the fostering, education, and training of specialized human resources under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 36 (Support for promoting offshore wind power supply chains) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may establish and implement policies necessary to promote offshore wind power supply chains. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall establish and implement the following financial policy measures to promote offshore wind power supply chains under paragraph (1): <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Raising financial resources to promote offshore wind power supply chains;
  2. Providing financial support, such as investments and loans, to offshore wind power project developers;
  3. Developing financial products to promote offshore wind power supply chains.
  (3) The State or local governments may provide tax support to offshore wind power-related project developers conducting projects in accordance with policies formulated under paragraph (1), as prescribed by tax-related statutes such as the Act on Restriction on Special Cases Concerning Taxation.

Article 37 (Creation and operation of offshore wind power demonstration complexes) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may create and operate offshore wind power demonstration complexes to verify technologies of offshore wind power companies, in order to promote the offshore wind power industry and strengthen the competitiveness of offshore wind power companies. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate and operate part of a power generation zone as a demonstration complex following deliberation and resolution by the Committee, in order to demonstrate offshore wind power technologies developed under Article 34(1). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may entrust the operation of offshore wind power demonstration complexes to relevant institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree, including public institutions under the Act on the Management of Public Institutions; and in such cases, the Minister may subsidize all or part of the associated costs. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (4) Where it is deemed necessary for the operation of an offshore wind power demonstration complex, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may select the relevant institution under paragraph (3) as an offshore wind power project developer, as prescribed by Presidential Decree, notwithstanding Article 24(1) and (2). <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (5) An offshore wind power project developer selected under paragraph (4) may trade electricity in the power market under Article 31(1) of the Electric Utility Act.
  (6) Matters necessary for the site selection for offshore wind power demonstration complexes and operation thereof under paragraphs (1) and (2) shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 38 (Designation of specialized research institutions) (1) To support research and development, commercialization, and other related activities for offshore wind power technologies, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may designate research institutes, laboratories, or organizations related to offshore wind power as specialized research institutions. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may subsidize all or part of the costs required to support specialized research institutions designated under paragraph (1) in terms of research and development, commercialization, etc. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (3) Where a specialized research institution designated under paragraph (1) falls under any of the following cases, the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may revoke the designation thereof; provided, in cases falling under subparagraph 1, such designation shall be revoked: <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  1. Where it has been designated by fraud or other improper means;
  2. Where it no longer meets the requirements for designation under paragraph (4).
  (4) Matters necessary for the requirements and procedures for designation, or revocation of designation, of specialized research institutions shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 39 (Promotion of international cooperation) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment shall monitor international trends in the offshore wind power industry and promote international cooperation. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may conduct projects, such as international exchanges of related technology and human resources and international  joint research and development, in order to promote international cooperation in the offshore wind power industry. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 40 (Support for harbor facilities and hinterland facilities) (1) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment and the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall endeavor to provide support for harbor facilities, hinterland facilities, vessels, etc. necessary for the construction and post-management of offshore wind power facilities so that offshore wind power projects can be smoothly promoted. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) The State shall make preferential efforts to create specialized complexes for the offshore wind power industry within local governments having jurisdiction over preliminary zones or power generation zones under Article 45 of the Act on Special Measures to Strengthen Competitiveness and Stabilize Supply Chain of Materials, Components, and Equipment Industries, and shall endeavor to provide support for the introduction, construction, and operation of vessels necessary for the construction and post-management of offshore wind power facilities, so as to ensure the smooth promotion of offshore wind power projects.

Article 41 (Support for fisheries and related industries) (1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may formulate policies necessary for the sustainable development of the fisheries industry and the systematic management of marine space, taking into account the impact of offshore wind power projects on the oceans and fisheries sector, and may formulate administrative and financial support measures necessary therefor.
  (2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may conduct surveys and research on the impacts of offshore wind power projects on the fisheries industry, marine environment, marine traffic, etc. in order to formulate policies under paragraph (1).
  (3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may entrust surveys and research, etc. under paragraph (2) to specialized institutions and organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree.

Article 42 (Special cases concerning application of fees for occupancy and use of public waters) (1) Notwithstanding Article 13(1) of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may differently determine the criteria for calculating occupancy fees and use fees for public waters with respect to offshore wind power facilities under this Act by public notice of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
  (2) Notwithstanding Article 7(1)4 of the Act on the Special Account for Traffic Facilities, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall allocate the occupancy fees and use fees for offshore wind power projects in the Exclusive Economic Zone under Article 2 of the Act on the Exclusive Economic Zone and the Continental Shelf, out of the occupancy fees and use fees under the jurisdiction of the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries pursuant to Article 13(2) of the Public Waters Management and Reclamation Act, as financial resources for the Fisheries Development Fund under Article 46 of the Framework Act on Fisheries and Fishing Villages Development.

Article 43 (Support for energy transition) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may formulate, implement, and provide support for policies necessary to convert coal-fired power plants into offshore wind power projects in order to contribute to the reduction of greenhouse gases in the power generation sector and the realization of carbon neutrality. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 44 (Reporting and inspection) (1) Where the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment deems it necessary for the installation of offshore wind power facilities and the creation and management of power generation zones, he or she may require an offshore wind power project developer to make necessary reports or order the submission of materials as prescribed by Decree of the Ministry of Climate, Energy and Environment, and may have public officials under his or her jurisdiction visit offices or places of business of the relevant developer, or other necessary places to inspect matters regarding its business or question interested persons. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Where an inspection under paragraph (1) is to be conducted, an inspection plan regarding the date and time, purpose, and details of the inspection, etc. shall be notified to the relevant project developer at least 7 days prior to the inspection; provided, this shall not apply in cases of urgency or where it is deemed that the purpose of the inspection cannot be achieved due to destruction of evidence, etc. if the inspection plan is notified in advance.
  (3) The relevant public official who makes a visit under paragraph (2) shall carry an identification verifying his or her authority and present it to interested persons.

Article 45 (Restriction on leakage of marine site information) No person shall commit any of the following acts with respect to data or information related to the results of marine exploration and surveys and marine site information:
  1. Acquiring, using, or disclosing data, etc. under Article 13 by improper means;
  2. Leaking data, etc. under Article 13 for the purpose of acquiring any unjust gain, or causing a third party to use such leaked data, etc.;
  3. The act of a person provided with data under Article 25(2) using such data for any purpose other than the purpose of provision, or transferring or providing the data to a third party.

Article 46 (Entrustment of business affairs) The Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment may entrust part of the business affairs under this Act to an institution or organization with expertise in or relevance to such affairs, as prescribed by Presidential Decree. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>

Article 47 (Duty of confidentiality) Any of the following persons shall neither disclose or divulge any confidential information related to offshore wind power that he or she has learned in the course of performing his or her duties, nor use such information for any purpose other than for the enforcement of this Act:
  1. A current or former member of the Committee;
  2. A current or former public official of the task force;
  3. A current or former executive officer or employee of an institution, corporation, or organization dispatched to the task force;
  4. A current or former executive officer or employee of an institution, corporation, or organization entrusted with business affairs under Article 46.

Article 48 (Legal fiction as public officials in application of penalty provisions) Any of the following persons shall be deemed a public official for the purpose of Articles 129 through 132 of the Criminal Act:
  1. A member commissioned under Article 7(2)2, who is not a public official;
  2. An executive officer or employee dispatched to the task force under Article 10(2), who is not a public official;
  3. An executive officer or employee of an institution or organization engaged in business affairs entrusted under Article 46.

CHAPTER VII PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 49 (Penalty provisions) (1) Any person who violates Article 45 shall be subject to imprisonment with labor for not more than 5 years or a fine not exceeding 200 million won.
  (2) Any person who has formulated an implementation plan under Article 25(1) and obtains approval thereof by fraud or other improper means shall be subject to imprisonment with labor for not more than 3 years or a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
  (3) Any person who has submitted relevant documents under Article 27(2), prepared by fraud or other improper means, and obtains approval of the relevant implementation plan shall be subject to imprisonment with labor for not more than 3 years or a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
  (4) Any person who divulges secrets in violation of Article 47 shall be subject to imprisonment with labor for not more than 3 years or a fine not exceeding 30 million won.
  (5) Any person who commences a development project without reporting to the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment in violation of Article 30(1) shall be subject to imprisonment with labor for not more than one year or a fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (6) Any person who uses power generation facilities and equipment without obtaining authorization for completion of construction under Article 31(2), in violation of Article 31(5), shall be subject to a fine not exceeding 3 million won.

Article 50 (Joint penalty provisions) If the representative of a corporation or an agent or employee of, or any other person employed by, the corporation or an individual commits any violation described in Article 49 in connection with the business affairs of the corporation or individual, the corporation or individual shall be subject to a fine prescribed in that Article in addition to the punishment imposed on the offender; provided, this shall not apply where such corporation or individual has not neglected to exercise due care and supervision regarding the relevant affairs to prevent such offense.

Article 51 (Administrative fines) (1) Any person who refuses, interferes with, or evades an inspection by the Minister of Climate, Energy and Environment under Article 44(1) shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 10 million won. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2025>
  (2) Any person who fails to make a report or submit data under Article 44(1) or who makes a false report or submits false data shall be subject to an administrative fine not exceeding 5 million won.

